 "api_error_status": null,
 "result": "**Mechanism:** a `contextvars.ContextVar` plus werkzeug `LocalProxy` objects.\n\n**In `src/tessel/globals.py`:**\n- `_app_var` (line 40) is a `ContextVar[AppContext]` that holds the current context.\n- Each global is a `LocalProxy` onto `_app_var`. A `LocalProxy` looks up the context var each time it's used, then reads an attribute from the result:\n  - `app_ctx` is the `AppContext` itself.\n  - `active_app` is `AppContext.app`, the `Tessel` instance.\n  - `g` is `AppContext.g`, an `_AppCtxGlobals` object.\n  - `request` is `AppContext.request`, a `Request` from `src/tessel/wrappers.py`.\n  - `session` is `AppContext.session`.\n- If nothing is bound, the proxies raise an error with the \"Working outside of application context\" or \"…request context\" message. For `request` and `session`, that applies when there's an app context but no request.\n\n**In `src/tessel/ctx.py`:**\n- The `AppContext` class (line 260) binds itself in `push()` with `_cv_token = _app_var.set(self)` (line 433).\n- `pop()` calls `_app_var.reset(self._cv_token)` (line 498), which restores the previous context.\n\nBecause the binding lives in a `ContextVar`, each thread or async task sees its own context.\n\nThe request and app contexts are merged into the single `AppContext`. The old `request_ctx` name is a deprecated alias for `app_ctx`.",
